<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="L-methionine degradation I (to L-homocysteine)" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="METHIONINE-DEG1-PWY" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: This pathway is on the list of Ubiquitous Plant Pathways (UPP) that was generated for the COMET project, which includes pathways that are thought to be present in all or most land plants. All UPP pathways are automatically imported into databases regardless of Pathologic predictions. &lt;A href=&quot;https://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; In the degradation pathway shown here, |FRAME: MET| is first metabolized to|FRAME: S-ADENOSYLMETHIONINE| (SAM), a compound that mediates most biological methylations. In the second reaction, the &lt;i&gt;S&lt;/i&gt;-methyl group is transferred to other acceptors in a transmethylation step. The product, |FRAME: ADENOSYL-HOMO-CYS|, is then hydrolyzed to |FRAME: HOMO-CYS| and |FRAME: ADENOSINE|. |FRAME: HOMO-CYS| is a branch point. In rats and humans approximately half of the homocysteine formed is remethylated back to methionine via two salvage pathways, one involving |FRAME: MONOMER-9225| (EC 2.1.1.13), and the other involving |FRAME:CPLX-5601| (EC 2.1.1.5) (see |FRAME:ADENOSYLHOMOCYSCAT-PWY|). The remainder of the homocysteine is degraded further. The first step is the condensation with serine to form |FRAME:L-CYSTATHIONINE|. |FRAME:CPLX-5163| catalyzes cleavage of the C-&amp;gamma;-S bond of |FRAME:L-CYSTATHIONINE|, yielding |FRAME:CYS|, |FRAME:2-OXOBUTANOATE| and |FRAME:AMMONIA| in a transsulfuration reaction (see |FRAME:HOMOCYSDEGR-PWY|) |CITS: [3309559][15539209]|.</Description>
  <Authors>
    <Author name="SRI" />
    <Author name="caspi" />
    <Author name="wagg" />
  </Authors>
